The rare vowel {{tlit|ojp|i<sub>2</sub>}} almost always occurred at the end of a morpheme.
Most occurrences of {{tlit|ojp|e<sub>1</sub>}}, {{tlit|ojp|e<sub>2</sub>}} and {{tlit|ojp|o<sub>1</sub>}} were also at the end of a morpheme.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=49}}

The {{tlit|ja|mokkan}} typically did not distinguish voiced from voiceless consonants, and wrote some syllables with characters that had fewer strokes and were based on older Chinese pronunciations imported via the Korean peninsula.
For example,
* {{tlit|ojp|ki<sub>1</sub>}} was written with the character {{lang|zh|支}}, pronounced *{{IPA|kje}} in [[Old Chinese]] and {{tlit|ltc|tsye}} in Middle Chinese, and
* {{tlit|ojp|to<sub>2</sub>}} was written with the character {{lang|zh|止}}, pronounced *{{IPA|tjəʔ}} in Old Chinese and {{tlit|ltc|tsyi}} in Middle Chinese.{{sfn|Inukai|2024|pp=466–468}}

