The distinction between ''mo<sub>1</sub>'' and ''mo<sub>2</sub>'' was seen only in ''Kojiki'' and vanished afterwards.
The distribution of syllables suggests that there may have once been *''po<sub>1</sub>'', *''po<sub>2</sub>'', *''bo<sub>1</sub>'' and *''bo<sub>2</sub>''.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=30}}

===Accent===
Although modern [[Japanese dialects]] have [[pitch-accent language|pitch-accent]] systems, pitch distinctions were usually not shown in {{tlit|ja|man'yōgana}}. However, in one part of the ''Nihon Shoki'', the Chinese characters appeared to have been chosen to represent pitch patterns similar to those recorded in the ''[[Ruiju Myōgishō]]'', a dictionary that was compiled in the late 11th century. In that section, a low-pitch syllable was represented by a character with the [[Middle Chinese]] level tone and a high pitch was represented by a character with one of the other three [[four tones (Middle Chinese)|Middle Chinese tones]]. A similar division was used in the [[tone pattern]]s of [[Chinese poetry]], which were emulated by Japanese poets in the late [[Asuka period]]. Thus, it appears that the Old Japanese accent system was similar to that of Early Middle Japanese.{{sfn|Miyake|2003|pp=37–39}}

===Phonotactics===
Old Japanese words consisted of one or more [[open syllable]]s of the form (C)V, subject to additional restrictions:
* Words did not begin with ''r'' or the voiced obstruents ''b'', ''d'', ''z'', and ''g'', with the exception of a few loanwords.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=43}}
* A bare vowel did not occur except for word-initially: vowel sequences were not permitted.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=39}}

In 1934, [[Hideyo Arisaka]] proposed a set of phonological restrictions permitted in a single morpheme. Arisaka's Law states that ''-o<sub>2</sub>'' was generally not found in the same morpheme as ''-a'', ''-o<sub>1</sub>'' or ''-u''.
Some scholars have interpreted that as a vestige of earlier [[vowel harmony]], but it is very different from patterns that are observed in, for example, the [[Turkic languages]].{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=44}}

===Morphophonemics===
Two adjacent vowels fused to form a new vowel when a consonant was lost within a morpheme, or a compound was lexicalized as a single morpheme.
The following fusions occurred:

;''i<sub>1</sub>'' + ''a'' → ''e<sub>1</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|saki<sub>1</sub>}} 'bloom' + {{tlit|ojp|ari}} 'exist' → {{tlit|ojp|sake<sub>1</sub>ri}} 'be blooming'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=532}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=48}}
:* {{tlit|ojp|ki<sub>1</sub>}} 'wear' + {{tlit|ojp|aru}} 'be.{{sc|ATT}}' → {{tlit|ojp|ke<sub>1</sub>ru}} 'wear.{{sc|FIN}}'{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=81}}
:Further examples are provided by verbs ending with the past tense suffix -{{tlit|ojp|ki<sub>1</sub>}} and the verbal auxiliaries {{tlit|ojp|amu}} 'conjecture' or {{tlit|ojp|ari}} 'exist':{{sfn|Russell|2003|pp=521–522}}
:* {{tlit|ojp|tir-i-ki<sub>1</sub> 'fall, scatter.{{sc|INF}}.{{sc|PST}}'}} + {{tlit|ojp|am-u}} → {{tlit|ojp|tirike<sub>1</sub>mu}} '(it) has surely fallen'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=531}}
:* {{tlit|ojp|ari-ki<sub>1</sub>}} 'exist.{{sc|INF}}.{{sc|PST}}' + {{tlit|ojp|ar-i}} → {{tlit|ojp|arike<sub>1</sub>ri}} 'it existed'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=531}}
;''i<sub>1</sub>'' + ''o<sub>2</sub>'' → ''e<sub>1</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|utusi}} 'real' + {{tlit|ojp|oyomi<sub>1</sub>}} 'person' → {{tlit|ojp|utuse<sub>1</sub>mi<sub>1</sub>}} 'living person'{{sfn|Erickson|2003|p=499}}{{efn|An alternative form, {{tlit|ojp|utuso<sub>2</sub>mi<sub>1</sub>}}, obtained by deleting the vowel ''-i'', is also attested.{{sfn|Erickson|2003|p=499}}}}
;''a'' + ''i'' → ''e<sub>2</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|naga}} 'long' + {{tlit|ojp|iki<sub>1</sub>}} 'breath' → {{tlit|ojp|nage<sub>2</sub>ki<sub>1</sub>}} 'sigh'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=532}}
:* {{tlit|ojp|taka}} 'high' + {{tlit|ojp|iti}} 'market' → {{tlit|ojp|take<sub>2</sub>ti}} (place name){{sfn|Russell|2003|p=532}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=46}}
;''o<sub>2</sub>'' + ''i'' → ''e<sub>2</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|tono}} 'palace' + {{tlit|ojp|iri}} 'enter' → {{tlit|ojp|toneri}} 'attendant'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=532}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=81}}
;''o<sub>2</sub>'' + ''i'' → ''i<sub>2</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|opo}} 'big' + {{tlit|ojp|isi}} 'rock' → {{tlit|ojp|opi<sub>2</sub>si}} 'big rock'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=532}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=81}}
;''u'' + ''i'' → ''i<sub>2</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|waku}} 'young' + {{tlit|ojp|iratuko<sub>1</sub>}} 'term of veneration (male)' → {{tlit|ojp|waki<sub>2</sub>ratuko<sub>1</sub>}} (title){{sfn|Russell|2003|p=532}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=81}}
;''u'' + ''a'' → ''o<sub>1</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|kazu}} 'number' + {{tlit|ojp|ape<sub>2</sub>}} 'to join' → {{tlit|ojp|kazo<sub>1</sub>pe<sub>2</sub>}} 'to count'{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=48}}{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=531}}
;''u'' + ''o'' → ''o<sub>1</sub>''
:* {{tlit|ojp|situ}} 'ancient type of native weaving' + {{tlit|ojp|ori}} 'weaving' → {{tlit|ojp|sito<sub>1</sub>ri}} 'native weaving'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=532}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=48}}

Adjacent vowels belonging to different morphemes, or pairs of vowels for which none of the above fusions applied, were reduced by deleting one or other of the vowels.{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=523}}
Most often, the first of the adjacent vowels was deleted:{{sfn|Unger|2000|p=662}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=55}}
* {{tlit|ojp|to<sub>2</sub>ko<sub>2</sub>}} 'eternal' + {{tlit|ojp|ipa}} 'rock' → {{tlit|ojp|to<sub>2</sub>ki<sub>1</sub>pa}} 'eternal rock; everlasting'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=528}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=40}}
* {{tlit|ojp|ama}} 'heaven' + {{tlit|ojp|ori}} 'descend' → {{tlit|ojp|amori}} 'descend from heaven'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=525}}
The exception to this rule occurred when the first of the adjacent vowels was the sole vowel of a monosyllabic morpheme (usually a [[clitic]]), in which case the other vowel was deleted:{{sfn|Unger|2000|p=662}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=55}}
* {{tlit|ojp|mi<sub>1</sub>}} (honorific) + {{tlit|ojp|uma}} 'horse' → {{tlit|ojp|mi<sub>1</sub>ma}} 'honourable horse'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=530}}
* {{tlit|ojp|ko<sub>1</sub>}} 'child, egg' + {{tlit|ojp|umu}} 'birth' → {{tlit|ojp|ko<sub>1</sub>mu}} 'give birth, lay an egg'{{sfn|Russell|2003|p=529}}
Cases where both outcomes are found are attributed to different analyses of morpheme boundaries:{{sfn|Unger|2000|p=662}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=40}}
* {{tlit|ojp|waga}} 'my' + {{tlit|ojp|ipe<sub>1</sub>}} 'house' → {{tlit|ojp|wagi<sub>1</sub>pe<sub>1</sub>}} 'my house'
* {{tlit|ojp|wa}} 'I' + {{tlit|ojp|ga}} {{sc|GEN}} + {{tlit|ojp|ipe<sub>1</sub>}} 'house' → {{tlit|ojp|wagape<sub>1</sub>}} 'my house'

=== Pre-Old Japanese ===
[[Internal reconstruction]] suggests that the stage preceding Old Japanese had fewer consonants and vowels.{{sfn|Miyake|2003|pp=73, 80–81}}

==== Consonants ====
Internal reconstruction suggests that the Old Japanese voiced obstruents, which always occurred in medial position, arose from the weakening of earlier nasal syllables before voiceless obstruents:{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=73}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=42–43}}
* ''b'' {{IPA|/ᵐb/}} < *-mVp-, *-nVp-: e.g. {{tlit|ojp|ami<sub>1</sub>}} 'net' + {{tlit|ojp|pi<sub>1</sub>ki<sub>1</sub>}} 'pull' → {{tlit|ojp|abi<sub>1</sub>ki<sub>1</sub>}} 'trawling'
* ''d'' {{IPA|/ⁿd/}} < *-mVt-, *-nVt-: e.g. {{tlit|ojp|yama}} 'mountain' + {{tlit|ojp|mi<sub>1</sub>ti}} 'path' → {{tlit|ojp|yamadi}} 'mountain path'
* ''z'' {{IPA|/ⁿz/}} < *-mVs-, *-nVs-: e.g. {{tlit|ojp|mura}} 'village' + {{tlit|ojp|nusi}} 'master' → {{tlit|ojp|murazi}} (title)
* ''g'' {{IPA|/ᵑɡ/}} < *-mVk-, *-nVk-
In some cases, such as {{tlit|ojp|tubu}} 'grain', {{tlit|ojp|kadi}} 'rudder' and {{tlit|ojp|pi<sub>1</sub>za}} 'knee', there is no evidence for a preceding vowel, which leads some scholars to posit final nasals at the earlier stage.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=43}}

Some linguists suggest that Old Japanese ''w'' and ''y'' derive, respectively, from *b and *d at some point before the oldest inscriptions in the 6th century.{{sfn|Miyake|2003|pp=71–73}} [[Southern Ryukyuan languages|Southern Ryukyuan varieties]] such as [[Miyako language|Miyako]], [[Yaeyama language|Yaeyama]] and [[Yonaguni language|Yonaguni]] have {{IPA|/b/}} corresponding to Old Japanese ''w'', but only Yonaguni (at the far end of the chain) has {{IPA|/d/}} where Old Japanese has ''y'':{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|p=195}}
* {{tlit|rys|ba}} 'I' and {{tlit|rys|bata}} 'stomach' corresponding to Old Japanese {{tlit|ojp|wa}} and {{tlit|ojp|wata}}
* Yonaguni {{tlit|yoi|da}} 'house', {{tlit|yoi|du}} 'hot water' and {{tlit|yoi|dama}} 'mountain' corresponding to Old Japanese {{tlit|ojp|ya}}, {{tlit|ojp|yu}} and {{tlit|ojp|yama}}
However, many linguists, especially in Japan, argue that the Southern Ryukyuan voiced stops are local innovations,{{sfn|Vovin|2010|pp=36–44}} adducing a variety of reasons.{{sfn|Pellard|2024|pp=45–49}}

Some supporters of *b and *d also add *z and *g, which both disappeared in Old Japanese, for reasons of symmetry.{{sfn|Unger|2000|p=666}}
However, there is very little Japonic evidence for them.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=43}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|pp=68–71}}

==== Vowels ====
As seen in {{section link||Morphophonemics}}, many occurrences of the rare vowels ''i<sub>2</sub>'', ''e<sub>1</sub>'', ''e<sub>2</sub>'' and ''o<sub>1</sub>'' arise from fusion of more common vowels.
Similarly, many nouns having independent forms ending in ''-i<sub>2</sub>'' or ''-e<sub>2</sub>'' also have bound forms ending in a different vowel, which are believed to be older.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}
For example, {{tlit|ojp|sake<sub>2</sub>}} 'rice wine' has the form {{tlit|ojp|saka-}} in compounds such as {{tlit|ojp|sakaduki}} 'sake cup'.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=80}}
The following alternations are the most common:
* ''i<sub>2</sub>''/''u-'': {{tlit|ojp|kami<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|kamu-}} 'god, spirit',{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=80}} {{tlit|ojp|mi<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|mu-}} 'body',{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|p=134}} {{tlit|ojp|nagi<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|nagu-}} 'a calm'.{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|p=134}} {{tlit|ojp|tuki<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|tuku-}} 'moon', {{tlit|ojp|kuki<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|kuku-}} 'stalk'.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=85}}
* ''i<sub>2</sub>''/''o<sub>2</sub>-'': {{tlit|ojp|ki<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|ko<sub>2</sub>-}} 'tree',{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=80}} {{tlit|ojp|yomi<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|yomo<sub>2</sub>-}} 'Hades',{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}
* ''e<sub>2</sub>''/''a-'': {{tlit|ojp|me<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|ma-}} 'eye',{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}} {{tlit|ojp|ame<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|ama-}} 'heaven', {{tlit|ojp|ame<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|ama-}} 'rain', {{tlit|ojp|kage<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|kaga-}} 'shade',{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|p=133}} {{tlit|ojp|ke<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|ka-}} 'day, sun', {{tlit|ojp|tume<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|tuma-}} 'nail, hoof', {{tlit|ojp|take<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|taka-}} 'bamboo'.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=85}}
The widely accepted analysis of this situation is that the most common Old Japanese vowels ''a'', ''u'', ''i<sub>1</sub>'' and ''o<sub>2</sub>'' reflect earlier *a, *u, *i and *ə respectively, and the other vowels reflect fusions of these vowels:{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=44, 48}}
* ''i<sub>2</sub>'' < *ui, *əi
* ''e<sub>1</sub>'' < *ia, *iə
* ''e<sub>2</sub>'' < *ai
* ''o<sub>1</sub>'' < *ua, *uə
Thus the above independent forms of nouns can be derived from the bound form and a suffix *-i.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=80}}
The origin of this suffix is debated, with one proposal being the ancestor of the obsolescent particle {{tlit|ojp|i}} (whose function is also uncertain), and another being a weakened consonant (suggested by proposed Korean cognates).{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=45, 131–132}}

There are also alternations suggesting ''e<sub>2</sub>'' < *əi, such as {{tlit|ojp|se<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|so<sub>2</sub>-}} 'back' and {{tlit|ojp|me<sub>2</sub>}}/{{tlit|ojp|mo<sub>2</sub>-}} 'bud'.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=45}}
Some authors believe that they belong to an earlier layer than ''i<sub>2</sub>'' < *əi, but others reconstruct two central vowels *ə and *ɨ, which merged everywhere except before *i.{{sfn|Erickson|2003|p=499}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=45–47}}
Other authors attribute the variation to different reflexes in different dialects and note that *əi yields ''e'' in Ryukyuan languages.{{sfn|Unger|2000|p=661}}

Some instances of word-final ''e<sub>1</sub>'' and ''o<sub>1</sub>'' are difficult to analyse as fusions, and some authors postulate *e and *o to account for such cases.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=47–48}}
A few alternations, as well as comparisons with [[Eastern Old Japanese]] and Ryukyuan languages, suggest that *e and *o also occurred in non-word-final positions at an earlier stage but were raised in such positions to ''i<sub>1</sub>'' and ''u'', respectively, in central Old Japanese.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=47–48, 153}}{{sfn|Vovin|2010|pp=32–36}}
The mid vowels are also found in some early {{tlit|ja|mokkan}} and in some modern Japanese dialects.{{sfn|Osterkamp|2017|pp=46–48}}

== Morphology ==
Old Japanese nominals tend to have simple morphology and little fusion, in contrast to the complex inflectional morphology of verbs.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=83}}
Japanese at all stages has used prefixes with both nouns and verbs, but Old Japanese also used prefixes for grammatical functions later expressed using suffixes.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=79}}
This is atypical of SOV languages, and may suggest that the language was in the final stage of a transition from a SVO typology.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=79}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=530}}

=== Nominals ===
==== Pronouns ====
Old Japanese include [[personal pronoun]]s, [[demonstrative]]s and an isolated [[interrogative pronoun]] {{tlit|ojp|nani}} 'what'.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=136}}
Many pronouns have both a short form and a longer form with attached {{tlit|ojp|-re}} of uncertain etymology.
If the pronoun occurs in isolation, the longer form is used.
The short form is used with genitive particles or in nominal compounds, but in other situations either form is possible.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=136–137}}

Personal pronouns are distinguished by taking the [[genitive case|genitive]] marker {{tlit|ojp|ga}}, in contrast to the marker {{tlit|ojp|no<sub>2</sub>}} used with demonstratives and nouns.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=138}}
* The first-[[grammatical person|person]] pronouns, {{tlit|ojp|a(re)}} and {{tlit|ojp|wa(re)}}, are used for the singular and plural respectively, though with some overlap. The {{tlit|ojp|wa-}} forms are also used [[reflexive pronoun|reflexively]], which suggests that {{tlit|ojp|wa}} was originally an [[indefinite pronoun]] and gradually replaced {{tlit|ojp|a}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=138}}
* The second-person pronoun is {{tlit|ojp|na(re)}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=136}}
* The third-person pronoun {{tlit|ojp|si}} is much less commonly used than the non-proximal demonstrative {{tlit|ojp|so<sub>2</sub>}} from which it was derived.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=138–139}}
* There is also an interrogative pronoun {{tlit|ojp|ta(re)}} and a reflexive pronoun {{tlit|ojp|ono<sub>2</sub>}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=136}}

Demonstratives often distinguish proximal (to the speaker) and non-proximal forms marked with {{tlit|ojp|ko<sub>2</sub>-}} and {{tlit|ojp|so<sub>2</sub>-}} respectively.
Many forms have corresponding interrogative forms {{tlit|ojp|i(du)-}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=139–140}}

