In Early Middle Japanese, the non-proximal {{tlit|ojp|so-}} forms were reinterpreted as hearer-based (medial), and the speaker-based forms were divided into proximal {{tlit|ojp|ko-}} forms and distal {{tlit|ojp|ka-}}/{{tlit|ojp|a-}} forms, yielding the three-way distinction that is still found in Modern Japanese.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=140, 246–247}}

==== Numerals ====
In later texts, such as the ''Man'yōshū'', numerals are sometimes written using Chinese logographs, which give no indication of pronunciation.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=285}}
