The forms for 50 and 70 are known only from Heian texts.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=344–345}}

There is a single example of a phonographically recorded compound number, in ''Bussokuseki'' 2:{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=344}}

{{interlinear|lang=ojp|indent=3
|mi<sub>1</sub>so-ti amar-i puta-tu no<sub>2</sub> katati
|thirty-CL exceed-INF two-CL GEN mark
|'thirty-two marks'}}

This example uses the [[classifier (linguistics)|classifier]]s {{tlit|ojp|-ti}} (used with tens and hundreds) and {{tlit|ojp|-tu}} (used with digits and hundreds).{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=354}}

The only attested ordinal numeral is {{tlit|ojp|patu}} 'first'.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=352–353}}
In [[Classical Japanese]], the other ordinal numerals have the same form as cardinals. This may also have been the case for Old Japanese, but there are no textual occurrences to settle the question.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=352}}

==== Classifiers ====
The [[classifier (linguistics)|classifier]] system of Old Japanese is much less developed than at later stages of the language, and classifiers are not obligatory between numerals and nouns.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=327}}
A few bound forms are attested phonographically: {{tlit|ojp|-tu}} (used with digits and hundreds), {{tlit|ojp|-ti}} (used with tens and hundreds), {{tlit|ojp|-ri}} (for people), {{tlit|ojp|-moto<sub>2</sub>}}, {{tlit|ojp|-pe<sub>1</sub>}} (for grassy plants) and {{tlit|ojp|-ri}} (for days).{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=354–364}}
Many ordinary nouns can also be used either freely or as classifiers.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=327}}

==== Prefixes ====
Old Japanese nominal prefixes include honorific {{tlit|ojp|mi-}}, intensive {{tlit|ojp|ma-}} from {{tlit|ojp|ma}} 'truth', diminutive or affectionate {{tlit|ojp|wo-}} and a prefix {{tlit|ojp|sa-}} of uncertain function.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=85–108}}

==== Suffixes ====
Old Japanese nominals have suffixes or particles to mark diminutives, plural number and case.
When multiple suffixes occur, case markers come last.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=109}} Unmarked nouns (but not pronouns) are neutral as to number.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=110}}
The main plural markers are the general-purpose {{tlit|ojp|ra}} and two markers restricted to animate nouns, {{tlit|ojp|do<sub>2</sub>mo<sub>2</sub>}} (limited to five words) and {{tlit|ojp|tati}}.{{sfn|Bentley|2012|p=195}}

The main case particles are{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=126}}

* [[accusative case|accusative]] {{tlit|ojp|wo}} marks objects (as in later Japanese) but also adverbials of duration.
* [[genitive case|genitive]] {{tlit|ojp|no<sub>2</sub>}} (unrestricted) and {{tlit|ojp|ga}} (restricted to people). In [[Late Middle Japanese]], {{tlit|ja|ga}} shifted to a [[nominative case]] marker.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=126–127}}
* [[dative case|dative]] or [[locative case|locative]] {{tlit|ojp|ni}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=145–149}}
* [[ablative case|ablative]] {{tlit|ojp|yo<sub>1</sub>ri}} ~ {{tlit|ojp|yo<sub>1</sub>}} ~ {{tlit|ojp|yuri}} ~ {{tlit|ojp|yu}} from {{tlit|ojp|yuri}} 'after(wards)'.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=134}} Only the form {{tlit|ja|yori}} survived in Early Middle Japanese.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=126}}
* [[comitative case|comitative]] {{tlit|ojp|to<sub>2</sub>}}

The subject of a sentence is usually not marked.{{sfn|Bentley|2001|p=259}}
There are a few cases in the ''Senmyō'' of subjects of active verbs marked with a suffix {{tlit|ojp|-i}}, which is thought to be an archaism that was obsolete in the Old Japanese period.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=126–129}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=131}}

=== Verbs ===
Old Japanese has a richer system of verbal suffixes than later forms of Japanese.{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|p=123}}
Old Japanese verbs use [[inflection]] for [[linguistic modality|modal]] and [[conjunction (grammar)|conjunctional]] purposes.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=53}}
Other categories, such as [[voice (grammar)|voice]], [[grammatical tense|tense]], [[grammatical aspect|aspect]] and [[grammatical mood|mood]], are expressed by using optional suffixed [[auxiliary verb|auxiliaries]], which are also inflected:{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=59}}

{{interlinear|lang=ojp|indent=3|abbreviations=MPST:modal past; CONCL:conclusive
| mayo<sub>1</sub>pi<sub>1</sub>-ki<sub>1</sub>-ni-ke<sub>1</sub>ri
| fray-come-PERF-MPST.CONCL
| 'had become frayed' (''Man'yōshū'' 14.3453){{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=51}}
}}

==== Inflected forms ====
As in later forms of Japanese, Old Japanese verbs have a large number of inflected forms.
In traditional Japanese grammar, they are represented by six forms ({{tlit|ja|katsuyōkei}}, {{lang|ja|活用形}}) from which all the others may be derived in a similar fashion to the [[principal parts]] used for [[Latin]] and other languages:{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=114–118}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|未然形|Mizenkei}} (irrealis)
:This form never occurs in isolation but only as a stem to which several particles and auxiliaries are attached.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=111–112}} This stem originated from resegmentation of an initial {{IPA|*a}} of several suffixes (auxiliary verbs) as part of the stem.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=112, 120–121}}{{sfn|Unger|2000|p=664}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|連用形|Ren'yōkei}} (adverbial, infinitive)
:This form is used to mark a predicate as coordinate with a following predicate.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=56–57}} It also serves as a stem for auxiliaries expressing tense and aspect, as well as suffixes {{tlit|ojp|-te}} and {{tlit|ojp|-tutu}} of adverbial subordinate forms.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=57, 109–111}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|終止形|Shūshikei}} (conclusive, predicative)
:This form is used as the main verb concluding a declarative sentence.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=53}} It is also used as a stem to which modal extensions, final particles, and some conjunctional particles are attached.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=123–124, 133}} The conclusive form merged with the attributive form by about 1600, but the distinction is preserved in the Ryukyuan languages and Hachijō.{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|pp=195, 207, 223–224}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|連体形|Rentaikei}} (attributive, adnominal)
:This form is used as the verb in a nominalized clause or a clause modifying a noun.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=54–55}} It also serves as a stem for the nominalizing suffix {{tlit|ojp|-aku}} and most conjunctional particles.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=113, 133}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|已然形|Izenkei}} (realis, exclamatory, subjunctive)
:This form is used as the main verb in an exclamatory sentence or as the verb in an adverbial clause.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=55}} It also serves as a stem for the particles {{tlit|ojp|ba}} (provisional) and {{tlit|ojp|do}} (concessive).{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=112–113}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|命令形|Meireikei}} (imperative)
:This form expresses the [[imperative mood]].{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=55}}

This system has been criticized because the six forms are not equivalent, with one being solely a combinatory stem, three solely word forms, and two being both.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=117}}
It also fails to capture some inflected forms.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=113, 117}}
However, five of the forms are basic inflected verb forms, and the system also describes almost all extended forms consistently.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=116–118}}

==== Conjugation classes ====
{{see also|Japanese godan and ichidan verbs}}
Old Japanese verbs are classified into eight conjugation classes that were originally defined for the classical Japanese of the late Heian period. In each class, the inflected forms showed a different pattern of rows of a kana table.
These rows correspond to the five vowels of later Japanese, but the discovery of the A/B distinction in Old Japanese showed a more refined picture.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=115}}

Three of the classes are grouped as consonant bases:{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=96}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|四段活用|Yodan}} (quadrigrade)
:This class of regular consonant-base verbs includes approximately 75% of verbs.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=96}} The class is so named because the inflections in later forms of Japanese span four rows of a {{tlit|ja|kana}} table, corresponding to four vowels. However, the discovery of the A/B distinction revealed that this class actually involved five different vowels in Old Japanese, with distinct vowels ''e<sub>1</sub>'' and ''e<sub>2</sub>'' in the exclamatory and imperative forms respectively.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=115}} The bases are almost all of the form (C)VC-, with the final consonant being ''p'', ''t'', ''k'', ''b'', ''g'', ''m'', ''s'' or ''r''.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=97}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|ナ行変格活用|Na-hen}} (''n''-irregular)
:The three ''n''-base verbs form a class of their own: {{tlit|ojp|sin-}} 'die', {{tlit|ojp|in-}} 'depart' and the perfective auxiliary {{tlit|ojp|-(i)n-}}. They are often described as a "hybrid" conjugation because the adnominal and exclamatory forms follow a similar pattern to vowel-base verbs.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=105}}
;{{wikt-lang|ja-Latn|ラ行変格活用|Ra-hen}} (''r''-irregular)
:The irregular ''r''-base verbs are {{tlit|ojp|ar-}} 'be, exist' and other verbs that incorporate it, as well as {{tlit|ojp|wor-}} 'be sitting', which became the existential verb {{tlit|ja|or-}} in later forms of Japanese.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=101–103}}

