The bigrade verbs seem to belong to a later layer than other verbs.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=120}}
Many ''e''-bigrade verbs are [[transitive verb|transitive]] or [[intransitive verb|intransitive]] counterparts of consonant-base verbs.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=118–119}}
In contrast, ''i''-bigrade verbs tend to be intransitive.{{sfn|Whitman|2008|p=164}}
Some bigrade bases also appear to reflect pre-Old-Japanese adjectives with vowel stems combined with an [[inchoative verb|inchoative]] *-i suffix:{{sfn|Unger|2000|p=665}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=119}}{{sfn|Whitman|2008|p=165}}
* *-a-i > ''-e<sub>2</sub>'', e.g. {{tlit|ojp|ake<sub>2</sub>-}} 'redden, lighten' vs {{tlit|ojp|aka}} 'red'.
* *-u-i > ''-i<sub>2</sub>'', e.g. {{tlit|ojp|sabi<sub>2</sub>-}} 'get desolate, fade' vs {{tlit|ojp|sabu-}} 'lonely'.
* *-ə-i > ''-i<sub>2</sub>'', e.g. {{tlit|ojp|opi<sub>2</sub>-}} 'get big, grow' vs {{tlit|ojp|opo-}} 'big'.

==== Copulas ====
Old Japanese has two copulas with limited and irregular conjugations:
{| class="wikitable"
|+ Old Japanese copulas{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=93–94}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=458–459}}
|-
! Infinitive !! Adnominal !! Gerund
|-
| {{tlit|ojp|ni}}
| {{tlit|ojp|no<sub>2</sub>}}
| {{tlit|ojp|nite}}
|-
| {{tlit|ojp|to<sub>2</sub>}}
| {{tlit|ojp|tu}}
|
|}
The {{tlit|ojp|tu}} form had a limited distribution in Old Japanese, and disappeared in Early Middle Japanese. In later Japanese, the {{tlit|ojp|nite}} form became {{tlit|ja|de}}, but these forms have otherwise endured to modern Japanese.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=94}}

==== Verbal prefixes ====
Japanese has used verbal prefixes conveying emphasis at all stages, but Old Japanese also has prefixes expressing grammatical functions, such as reciprocal or cooperative {{tlit|ojp|api<sub>1</sub>-}} (from {{tlit|ojp|ap-}} 'meet, join'), stative {{tlit|ojp|ari-}} (from {{tlit|ojp|ar-}} 'exist'), potential {{tlit|ojp|e<sub>2</sub>-}} (from {{tlit|ojp|e<sub>2</sub>-}} 'get') and prohibitive {{tlit|ojp|na-}}, which is often combined with a suffix {{tlit|ojp|-so<sub>2</sub>}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=79}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=512–515, 520–522, 530–534}}

==== Verbal auxiliaries ====

Old Japanese has a rich system of auxiliary elements that can be suffixed to verb stems and are themselves inflected, usually following the regular consonant-stem or vowel-stem paradigms, but never including the full range of forms found with full verbs.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=58–62}}
Many of these disappeared in later stages of the language.{{sfn|Bentley|2001|p=258}}

Tense and aspect are indicated by suffixes attached to the infinitive.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=109–111}}
The tense suffixes are:
* the simple past {{tlit|ojp|-ki<sub>1</sub>}} (conclusive), {{tlit|ojp|-si}} (adnominal), {{tlit|ojp|-sika}} (exclamatory).{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=62}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=827–830}} The variation may indicate an origin in multiple forms.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=121}} Indeed counterparts of {{tlit|ojp|-ki<sub>1</sub>}} are absent from Ryukyuan and weakly attested in Eastern Old Japanese.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=840–843}}
* the modal past or retrospective {{tlit|ojp|-ke<sub>1</sub>r-}}, a fusion of the simple past with {{tlit|ojp|ar-}} 'exist'.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=48, 121}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=879–881}}
* the past conjectural {{tlit|ojp|-ke<sub>1</sub>m-}}, a fusion of the simple past with the conjectural suffix {{tlit|ojp|-am-}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=48}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=833–834}}
The perfective suffixes are {{tlit|ojp|-n-}} and {{tlit|ojp|-te-}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=66–68, 123}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=844–866}}
During the [[Late Middle Japanese]] period, the tense and aspect suffixes were replaced with a single past-tense suffix {{tlit|ja|-ta}}, derived from {{tlit|ojp|-te}} + {{tlit|ojp|ar-}} 'exist' > {{tlit|ojp|-tar-}}.{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|p=123}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=69}}
However, the Hachijō language retains some of the early tense and aspect system, in particular reflexes of the old past suffix {{tlit|ojp|-si}} and the distinction between {{tlit|ojp|-te ar-}} and {{tlit|ojp|ar-}} forms.{{sfn|Kaneda|2025|pp=983–985}}

Other auxiliaries are attached to the irrealis stem:
* the negative {{tlit|ojp|-(a)n-}} and {{tlit|ojp|-(a)z-}} < *{{tlit|ojp|-(a)ni-su}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=69–72, 121}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=701–713}}
* the passive {{tlit|ojp|-(a)ye-}} and {{tlit|ojp|-(a)re-}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=63–64}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=745–758}}
* the causative {{tlit|ojp|-(a)sime-}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=63–64}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=771–776}}
* the honorific {{tlit|ojp|-(a)s-}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=62–63}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=758–771}}
* the iterative {{tlit|ojp|-(a)p-}} or {{tlit|ojp|-op-}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=738–745}}
* the conjectural or tentative {{tlit|ojp|-(a)m-}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=78}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=713–730}}
* the subjunctive {{tlit|ojp|-(a)masi-}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=78}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=606–610}}
The honorific and iterative ceased to be productive in Middle Japanese.{{sfn|Bentley|2001|p=258}}
During the Early Middle Japanese period, the causative {{tlit|ojp|-(a)sime-}} was replaced by {{tlit|ja|-(a)sase-}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=63, 236–238}}

===Adjectives===
Old Japanese adjectives were originally nominals and, unlike in later periods, can be used uninflected to modify following nouns.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=373–383}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=79–80}}
They can also take a suffix {{tlit|ojp|-ku}} (an adjectival copula), forming [[stative verb]]s conjugated in two classes:{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=80–81}}{{sfn|Bentley|2012|pp=197–198}}

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Conjugation of stative verbs{{sfn|Bentley|2012|p=198}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=82}}
|-
! Class
! Stem
! Infinitive !! Conclusive !! Adnominal !! Exclamatory
! Gloss
|-
! {{tlit|ojp|-ku}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kata}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kataku}}
| {{tlit|ojp|katasi}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kataki<sub>1</sub>}}
| {{tlit|ojp|katasa}}
| 'hard'
|-
! {{tlit|ojp|-siku}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kusi}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kusiku}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kusi}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kusiki<sub>1</sub>}}
| {{tlit|ojp|kusisa}}
| 'precious'
|}

The second class, with stems ending in {{tlit|ojp|-si}}, differs only in the conclusive form, whose suffix {{tlit|ojp|-si}} was dropped by [[haplology]].{{sfn|Bentley|2001|p=104}}
Adjectives of this class tend to express more subjective qualities.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=90}}
Many of them were formed from a verbal stem by the addition of a suffix {{tlit|ojp|-si}} of uncertain origin.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=91}}

Towards the end of the Old Japanese period, a more expressive conjugation was formed by adding the verb {{tlit|ojp|ar-}} 'be' to the infinitive, with the sequence {{tlit|ojp|-ua-}} reducing to {{tlit|ojp|-a-}}:{{sfn|Bentley|2012|pp=197–198}}

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Innovative conjugation of stative verbs{{sfn|Bentley|2001|p=138}}
|-
! Irrealis !! Infinitive !! Adnominal
! Gloss
|-
| {{tlit|ojp|katakara-}}
| {{tlit|ojp|katakari}}
| {{tlit|ojp|katakaru}}
| 'hard'
|}

Many [[adjectival noun (Japanese)|adjectival nouns]] of Early Middle Japanese were based on Old Japanese adjectives that were formed with suffixes {{tlit|ojp|-ka}}, {{tlit|ojp|-raka}} or {{tlit|ojp|-yaka}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=235}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=386–389}}

== Syntax ==
As in later forms of Japanese, Old Japanese word order is predominantly [[subject–object–verb]], with adjectives and adverbs preceding the nouns and verbs they modify and [[auxiliary verbs]] and [[grammatical particle|particles]] consistently appended to the main verb.{{sfn|Shibatani|1990|pp=122–123}}

{{interlinear|lang=ojp|indent=3|abbreviations=ADN:adnominal; CONCL:conclusive; PRES:presumptive
| ositeru nanipa no<sub>2</sub> mi<sub>1</sub>ya ni wa go<sub>2</sub> opo-ki<sub>1</sub>mi<sub>1</sub> kuni sir-asu rasi
| push.shine.ADN Naniwa GEN palace LOC 1S GEN great-lord land rule-HON.CONCL PRES
| 'In the shining Naniwa palace, my lord might rule the land.' (''Man'yōshū'' 6.933)
}}

Questions, exclamations and prohibitives are marked using [[Japanese particles|particles]], either in the middle of the sentence or at the end, while imperatives are marked by conjugating the verb.{{sfn|Bentley|2012|p=205}}

=== Focus construction ===

Old Japanese makes extensive use of a [[focus (linguistics)|focus]] construction, known as {{tlit|ja|kakari-musubi}} ('hanging-tying'), to express emphasis and questions. A focus particle marks a copular relation between the constituent preceding the particle and a predicate in the adnominal form, instead of the conclusive form usually found in declarative sentences.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=247, 250}}
The marked constituent is also typically fronted in comparison with its position in a corresponding declarative sentence.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=248–249}}
The semantic effect (though not the syntactic structure) is often similar to a [[cleft sentence]] in English:{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=248}}

{{interlinear|lang=ojp|indent=3|abbreviations=ADN:adnominal
| wa ga ko<sub>1</sub>puru ki<sub>1</sub>mi<sub>1</sub> '''so<sub>2</sub>''' ki<sub>1</sub>zo no<sub>2</sub> yo<sub>1</sub> ime<sub>2</sub> ni mi<sub>1</sub>-ye-'''turu'''
| 1S GEN love.ADN lord FOC last.night GEN night dream DAT see-PASS-PERF.ADN
| 'It was my beloved lord that I saw last night in a dream.' (''Man'yōshū'' 2.150)
}}

The particles involved are
* {{tlit|ojp|ya}}, marking the focus of a [[yes–no question]].{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=1122–1125}} The particle {{tlit|ojp|ya}} can also be used as a sentence-final marker of a yes–no question, in which case the verb is in the usual conclusive form.{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=1118–1122}}
* {{tlit|ojp|ka}}, marking the phrase containing the [[interrogative word]] of an [[wh-question|open question]] or the focus of a yes–no question.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=252}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=1127–1134}}
* {{tlit|ojp|so<sub>2</sub>}} ~ {{tlit|ojp|zo<sub>2</sub>}}, the usual declarative focus marker. By Early Middle Japanese, this had standardized as {{tlit|ojp|zo}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=253–254}}
* {{tlit|ojp|namo<sub>1</sub>}}, soliciting agreement, is rare in poetry but occurs in some prose works. By Early Middle Japanese, it had become {{tlit|ojp|namu}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=254–255}}
* {{tlit|ojp|ko<sup>2</sup>so<sup>2</sup>}}, marking a more emphatic focus.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=255}} In Early Middle Japanese, this particle occurs with a verb in the exclamatory form.{{sfn|Bentley|2001|p=258}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=1109}}

The focus construction is also common in [[Classical Japanese]], but disappeared after the Early Middle Japanese period.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=247}}
It is still found in Ryukyuan languages, but is much less common there than in Old Japanese.{{sfn|Pellard|2024|p=58}}

=== Nominalization ===

The nominal form of a verb, marked with {{tlit|ojp|-aku}}, forms a nominalized clause.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=57–58}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=687–692}}
The following example has a nominal clause within an adverbial clause marked with the infinitive form:{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=58}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=305, 1131}}

{{interlinear|lang=ojp|indent=3|abbreviations=ADN:adnominal; CONCL:conclusive; CONJC:conjectural; NMNL:nominalizer
| wa ga ko<sub>2</sub>ko<sub>2</sub>-da sino<sub>1</sub>p'''aku''' sira-n'''i''' poto<sub>2</sub>to<sub>2</sub>gi<sub>1</sub>su idu-pe<sub>1</sub> no yama wo naki<sub>1</sub> ka ko<sub>1</sub>yu ramu
| 1s GEN this-much yearn.NMNL know-NEG.INF cuckoo which-side GEN mountain ACC cry.INF INT cross.CONCL CONJC.ADN
| 'Not knowing how much I yearn, where will the cuckoo cross the mountains, crying?' (''Man'yōshū'' 19.4195)
}}

The nominal was heavily used in Old Japanese, both with verbs and adjectives, but ceased to be productive in Early Middle Japanese, when its function was taken over by the adnominal.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=57–58}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=687}}

==Dialects==
{{see also|Eastern Old Japanese}}
[[File:Nara and Azuma map.svg|thumb|right|upright=1.5|The capital (Nara) and the eastern provinces (hatched) in the 8th century]]
Although most Old Japanese writing represents the language of the [[Nara, Nara|Nara]] court in central Japan, some sources come from eastern Japan:{{sfn|Vovin|2020|pp=11–14}}{{sfn|Miyake|2003|p=159}}{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=23–24, 151}}{{sfn|Vovin|Ishisaki-Vovin|2022|pp=1–16}}
* 230 {{tlit|ja|azuma uta}} 'eastern songs', making up volume 14 of the ''Man'yōshū'',
* 93 (101 according to some authors) {{tlit|ja|sakimori uta}} 'borderguard songs' in volume 20 of the ''Man'yōshū'', and
* 9 songs in the ''Hitachi Fudoki'' (recorded 714–718, but the oldest extant manuscripts date from the late 17th century and show significant corruption{{sfn|Kupchik|2023|pp=4–5}}).

They record Eastern Old Japanese dialects,{{sfn|Kupchik|2023|pp=1–2, 4}} with several differences from central Old Japanese (also known as Western Old Japanese):
* There is no type A/B distinction on front vowels ''i'' and ''e'', but ''o<sub>1</sub>'' and ''o<sub>2</sub>'' are distinguished.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=152}}
* Pre-Old Japanese *ia yielded ''a'' in the east, where central Old Japanese has ''e<sub>1</sub>''.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=152}}
* The adnominal form of consonant-base verbs ended in {{tlit|ojp|-o<sub>1</sub>}}, whereas central Old Japanese had {{tlit|ojp|-u}} for both the adnominal and the conclusive forms.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|pp=152–153}} The modern Hachijō language distinguishes ''-o'' and ''-u'' in these forms.{{sfn|Kaneda|2025|pp=982–983}} A similar difference is preserved in Ryukyuan languages, suggesting that central Old Japanese had innovated by merging those endings.{{sfn|Bentley|2012|p=189}}
* The adnominal form of adjectives had {{tlit|ojp|-ke}} in place of central Old Japanese {{tlit|ojp|-ki<sub>1</sub>}}.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=153}}{{sfn|Vovin|2020|p=416}} This form is also preserved in Hachijō.{{sfn|Kaneda|2025|pp=982–983}}
* The imperative form of vowel-base verbs attached {{tlit|ojp|-ro<sub>2</sub>}}, instead of the {{tlit|ojp|-yo<sub>2</sub>}} used in central Old Japanese.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=154}} This difference has persisted into modern eastern and western dialects.{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=402}}
* There was a group of distinctive negative auxiliaries {{tlit|ojp|-(a)nap-}} and {{tlit|ojp|-(a)nan-}}, but they do not seem to be the source of the different negatives in the modern eastern and western [[Japanese dialects]].{{sfn|Frellesvig|2010|p=154}}
* There are a significant number of words borrowed from [[Ainu language|Ainu]].{{sfn|Vovin|2014|pp=13–15}}{{sfn|Vovin|Ishisaki-Vovin|2022|pp=35–38}}{{sfn|Kupchik|2023|pp=123–140}}

